## NAME
vtium - a terminal for the Linux virtual console

## SYNOPSIS
@SYNOPSIS@

## DESCRIPTION
vtium is a terminal that runs on a Linux virtual terminal (VT), one of the text screens you switch to with Ctrl+Alt and a function key. vtium takes the place of getty, the program that normally shows the login prompt there. vtium renders each frame itself through KMS/DRM, the kernel's graphics interface, so it shows modern fonts, color emoji, 24-bit color, and images.

systemd starts vtium on a VT through one of its units, and vtium then runs a login prompt or another command in each console. `vtium console enable` chooses the VTs that vtium runs on. See UNITS below.

vtium reads its settings from `/etc/vtium/vtium.conf` when it starts, and its command line wins over the file. vtium.conf(5) describes every setting. `vtium check-config` checks the file, and vtium-config(1) edits it.

## OPTIONS
These options exist only on the command line.

@OPTIONS@

Every top-level setting of vtium.conf is also an option: `--key=VALUE` or `--key VALUE`, and `--key` or `--no-key` for a switch. `--login -- COMMAND...` sets the command that each console runs. vtium.conf(5) lists the settings.

## COMMANDS
A command word as the first argument runs one of these commands instead of the console.

@COMMANDS@

## UNITS
= `vtium@.service`
Runs vtium on a VT in direct mode, as `vtium@ttyN.service`. vtium opens the displays and the keyboards itself, as root. The unit is also `autovt@.service`, the unit that logind starts on a VT you switch to. The unit conflicts with `getty@.service` on the same VT.

= `vtium-seat@.service`
Runs vtium on a VT in seat mode, where vtium takes the displays and the keyboards from logind through libseat. A build with libseat and logind ships this unit.

= `vtium-getty@.service`
Runs `vtium console fallback` when vtium fails on its VT. That starts a login prompt on the VT, unless the closed policy forbids it.

## KERNEL COMMAND LINE
= `vtium.enable=0`
Turns vtium off for one boot. vtium exits with status 1 before it reads anything else. Under the open policy, the getty fallback then starts a login prompt on the VT. Under the closed policy, no login prompt starts, because a login prompt would accept any keyboard.

= `systemd.unit=rescue.target`
Boots into rescue mode. vtium's units conflict with the rescue and emergency services, so vtium steps aside for them.

= `quiet`, `loglevel=`
Set the kernel's console log level. vtium shows a kernel message on screen only when that level lets the message through.

## HELPER PROGRAMS
vtium starts two helper programs, each in a process of its own, so that it never parses a font file or an image itself.

= `vtium-config serve`
The font helper, which reads the fonts that a font set names and renders their glyphs for the console. vtium starts it at the path that the `font-helper` setting names.

= `vtium-image`
The image helper, in a build with image support. vtium-image decodes the images that programs send, in a sandbox, under an account of its own that its sysusers file creates. vtium starts it with the `image-helper` setting's path. Nobody runs it by hand.

Two programs run inside a vtium console. vtium-clipboard(1), also called `vticlip`, copies text to the console's clipboard. vtium-gallery(1), in a build with image support, shows pictures as thumbnails.

## SIGNALS
= `SIGTERM`, `SIGINT`
End vtium, with status 0.

= `SIGRTMIN+1`
Turns off vtium's displays now, as `vtium blank` does.

= `SIGRTMIN+2`
Turns vtium's displays on again, as `vtium wake` does.

## EXIT STATUS
= 0
vtium ended on `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT`, or a console's command exited while the `oneshot` setting is on. `--help` and `--version` exit 0 too.

= 1
vtium stopped on an error. Its command line was wrong, or `vtium.enable=0` turned it off. A console's command could not start, or exited too quickly too many times. The VT hung up, or no display appeared within the time that the `display-wait` setting gives.

`vtium check-config` exits 0, 1 or 6, as its section above says.

## FILES
= `/etc/vtium/vtium.conf`
The settings and the sections. See vtium.conf(5).

= `/etc/vtium/fonts`
The font sets, each in a file `NAME.vtfrit`. Your own sets go in this folder itself. The sets vtium installs are in a folder for each category: `console`, `classic`, `code`, `nerd`, `scripts` and `small`. vtium looks for a set's name in this folder first, then in each category's folder.

= `/etc/vtium/fonts/layers`
The layers that font sets add, each in a file `NAME.vtlayer`, such as the emoji or Nerd Fonts' icons.

= `/usr/share/vtium/fonts`
The font files every vtium installs: bitmap Terminus at nine sizes, GNU Unifont, and Cullet Mono, an outline Terminus with Nerd Fonts' icons. The font sets `terminus` and `cullet-mono` name them.

= `/run/vtium`
The marker files that tell the getty fallback that a VT runs under the closed policy.

= `/usr/share/terminfo/x/xterm-vtium`
The terminfo entry that vtium sets as `TERM` in each console.

## SEE ALSO
vtium.conf(5), vtium-config(1), vtium-clipboard(1), vtium-gallery(1), systemd.service(5)
